; DECIMAL RENDERING OF A BIGNUM, WRITTEN IN OROBOROS — the first text program.
;
; It exists to answer a question string-operations.md could not: does a text
; program INDEX a string, or only fold it? That decides whether a string has to
; become an indexable table and pay UTF-32, or can stay the free monoid it is.
;
; THE ANSWER IS THAT IT FOLDS. Every string here is built by `concat` out of
; one-scalar strings — `η : Scalar → Scalar*`, the generator injection the free
; monoid's universal property is stated in terms of. Nothing is indexed, nothing
; needs a length, nothing compares two strings. Three operations, all derived.
;
; prints: string
; big-repr: both
;
; BOTH STORAGE CHOICES, which makes this the first program to drive the limb
; library end to end: `x % 10^8` is `rem-small`, `x / 10^8` is `div-small`, and
; `(= v 0)` and `(> nv 0)` are the limb-wise `cmp` — all three landed yesterday
; with only unit tests and a hand-written case behind them.
;
; skip: windows
;
; windows is skipped for a CAPABILITY reason and it is now a precise one: that
; host declares neither `concat` nor `string-of`, because it has no string type —
; a string there is a pointer to NUL-terminated bytes. It can do all the
; arithmetic below. What it needs is those two operations written as a library
; over `build`, which is the shape `emit/bignum.oro` already has.
;
;   run n = the decimal digits of (25 + n)!

; inputs: 0 5
; expect: 15511210043330985984000000 265252859812191058636308480000000

(export run)

; Eight decimal digits of a machine word, most significant first, built by
; PREPENDING — which is the monoid operation, and the reason no index is needed.
; `pad` keeps an interior group's leading zeros and drops the top group's; the
; two differ only in a guard, so there is one `again` (ADR 0015).
(def group (fn (d pad)
  (loop ((s "") (k 0) (r d))
    (>= k 8)                              s
    (and (not pad) (and (= r 0) (> k 0))) s
    else (again (concat (string-of (+ 48 (% r 10))) s) (+ k 1) (/ r 10)))))

; Least significant group first, each prepended, so the digits come out in order
; without anything being indexed or reversed. The trip bound is what makes it
; terminate provably: nothing can bound a big value's magnitude from its
; structure, so the program says how many groups there can be — `tree.oro`'s node
; cap again, where a capacity does not create the limit but makes it visible.
(def digits (fn (x)
  (loop ((s "") (v x) (i 0))
    (= v 0)   s
    (>= i 32) s
    else (let (/ v 100000000) (fn (nv)
           (again (concat (group (% v 100000000) (> nv 0)) s) nv (+ i 1)))))))

(sig render ((x (int 0 (pow 2 200)))) string)
(def render (fn (x) (if (= x 0) "0" (digits x))))

(sig fact ((n (int 0 40))) (int 0 (pow 2 200)))
(def fact (fn (n) (loop ((acc 1) (i 2)) (> i n) acc else (again (* acc i) (+ i 1)))))

(def run (fn (n) (render (fact (+ 25 n)))))
